<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Lament · 1913 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">El Cóndor Pasa</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="es">El cóndor pasa</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A tune from Peru, written in 1913 from old Andean music, that is now called Peru's second national anthem.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>none in the original orchestral piece; later words by many writers, among them Paul Simon (1970) and Armando Robles Godoy</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Daniel Alomía Robles, from traditional Andean music</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Peru; the folk music of the Andes</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Lima, Peru</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1913</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Lament</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>In copyright (the music, by Daniel Alomía Robles (1913; he died in 1942), is in the public domain, but the words later sung to it, Armando Robles Godoy's and Paul Simon's (1970), are not)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-condor-pasa">El Cóndor Pasa</a>&quot; (&quot;The Condor Passes&quot;) is an orchestral piece from the Peruvian zarzuela, or musical play, of the same name. The Peruvian composer Daniel Alomía Robles wrote its music in 1913, basing it on traditional Andean music, the folk music of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>; the script of the play, written in prose in two acts, was by Julio de La Paz, the pen name of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> dramatist Julio Baudouin. It was first performed in public at the Teatro Mazzi in Lima. On 3 May 1933 The Edward B. Marks Music Corp. registered a piano arrangement of the play's most famous melody at the Library of Congress.</p><p>The melody travelled far beyond the play. It has been estimated that more than 4,000 versions of it have been produced around the world, with some 300 different sets of words. In 1965 the American musician Paul Simon heard it played by the band Los <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where both were performing at the Théâtre de l'Est parisien. Simon became friendly with the band, toured with them and produced their first American album. When he asked to use the piece, the band's director Jorge Milchberg, who was registered as co-author of the arrangement and collected royalties for it, told him wrongly that it was a traditional Peruvian composition.</p><p>Simon &amp; Garfunkel recorded it as &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-condor-pasa">El Cóndor Pasa</a> (If I Could)&quot;, with English words credited to Simon over Los <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a>' instrumental track, on their 1970 album Bridge over Troubled Water. As a single in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> it reached number 18 on the Billboard pop chart and number 6 on the Easy Listening chart. In late 1970 Robles' son, the Peruvian filmmaker Armando Robles Godoy, brought a successful copyright suit against Simon, on the ground that his father had composed the piece and copyrighted it in the United States in 1933. He called the case almost friendly and the error an honest mistake, and later wrote new Spanish words for the song, taking Simon's version as a reference.</p><p>Many musicians have recorded it since, among them Peruvian guitarists and singers, the Argentine guitarist Eduardo Falú in a setting for solo guitar, and singers from Plácido Domingo to Celia Cruz. In 2004 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> declared the song part of its national cultural heritage, and it is now considered the second national anthem of Peru. Because Robles died in 1942, his original version has been in the public domain since 1 January 2013.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>In its original form the piece has no words: it is an orchestral melody drawn from the folk music of the Andes. The many later sets of words, in Spanish, English and other languages, are separate works by their own writers.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords" data-say>The words of this song are still in copyright, so they are not printed here. &#8220;What it says&#8221; above gives their theme in our own words, and the performance below lets you hear them.</p></section>
</div>
